PR #1541  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Undefined memory-mapped msip register format allowed unmasked writes to bits 31:1 with unpredictable implementation-specific behavior, and unspecified write-to-CSR reflection delay created IPI race windows.
  [BUG] drivers/clocksource/timer-clint.c:49-52  clint_send_ipi
      writel(1, ...) issues a 32-bit store of 0x00000001 to the MSIP register, unconditionally zeroing bits 31:1 on every IPI send. The security spec requires writes to be masked to bit 0 only (e.g., byte-access or read-modify-write), because bits 31:1 are implementation-defined and may carry side effects (routing, priority, microarchitectural state). On any implementation where bits 31:1 are not hardwired to zero, this destroys implementation-specific state on every IPI.
  [BUG] drivers/clocksource/timer-clint.c:54-57  clint_clear_ipi
      writel(0, ...) issues a 32-bit store of 0x00000000 to clear the MSIP register. Same unmasked-write pattern as clint_send_ipi: explicitly zeroes bits 31:1 without read-modify-write, assuming those bits have no architectural meaning. Violates the masking requirement in the spec.
  [BUG] drivers/irqchip/irq-aclint-sswi.c:22-25  aclint_sswi_ipi_send
      writel(0x1, ...) writes the 32-bit value 0x00000001 to the ACLINT SETSSIP register. This is the SSWI equivalent of CLINT MSIP — same unconditional upper-bits-zeroed pattern. Bits 31:1 of the SETSSIP register are implementation-defined and must not be unconditionally overwritten.
  [BUG] drivers/irqchip/irq-aclint-sswi.c:27-30  aclint_sswi_ipi_clear
      Two violations: (1) writel_relaxed(0x0, ...) writes 0x00000000 as a 32-bit store, unconditionally zeroing bits 31:1 of the SETSSIP register — same unmasked-write pattern. (2) The _relaxed variant omits the MMIO write barrier present in writel(), meaning the upper-bits-zeroing store may not be globally visible before subsequent ipi_mux_process() executes. This could allow a stale SSIP to appear still pending when virtual IPIs are processed.
  [RISK] drivers/clocksource/timer-clint.c:59-69  clint_ipi_interrupt
      Calls clint_clear_ipi() then immediately ipi_mux_process() with no read-back to verify the MSIP write completed before the MMIO domain. Assumes the writel(0) to MSIP is synchronously reflected in the local mip.MSIP bit before ipi_mux_process() iterates pending virtual IPIs. If the interconnect introduces write-posting delay, a concurrent MSIP write from another hart could be overwritten by the delayed clear, or ipi_mux_process() could see a stale pending state. No fence or read-back guards this assumption.
  [RISK] drivers/irqchip/irq-aclint-sswi.c:32-44  aclint_sswi_ipi_handle
      Double-clear pattern: csr_clear(CSR_IP, IE_SIE) clears SSIP via CSR, then aclint_sswi_ipi_clear() clears via writel_relaxed(0) MMIO. The _relaxed MMIO clear lacks an ordering barrier before ipi_mux_process(), so the SSWI register write may not be globally visible when virtual IPIs are processed. Additionally, the CSR clear assumes a 1:1 mapping between IE_SIE (a single-bit sip mask) and the SSWI MMIO register state — if the SETSSIP register's bits 31:1 independently affect sip bits, the CSR-only clear may not fully quiesce the interrupt source.
  [RISK] kernel/irq/ipi-mux.c:86-87  ipi_mux_send_mask
      Gate logic: if (!(pending & ibit) && ...) condition skips the physical IPI trigger when the vIPI bit was already pending. This assumes that a previously-set pending bit guarantees the physical IPI (MSIP write) has already been delivered and will be taken by the target CPU. If the MSIP→mip reflection has pipeline delay or the MSIP write was lost (e.g., due to implementation-specific upper-bit interpretation), the second vIPI is silently dropped — the gate sees 'already pending' but the first IPI was never actually observed by the target. No mechanism to detect or recover from lost physical IPIs.
  [RISK] kernel/irq/ipi-mux.c:36-52  ipi_mux_unmask
      When a vIPI is unmasked and its pending bit is already set, ipi_mux_send(smp_processor_id()) re-triggers the physical MSIP write path to the local CPU. Each invocation executes the unmasked writel(1, ...) which zeroes bits 31:1. In workloads with frequent mask/unmask cycles (e.g., CPU hotplug, irq affinity changes), repeated writel calls amplify any side effects of destroying implementation-defined state in bits 31:1. Safe on current hardware where those bits are hardwired; unsafe if they carry state.
  [OK] drivers/clocksource/timer-clint.c:164-218  clint_timer_init_dt
      Initialization function that maps CLINT MMIO base and sets clint_ipi_base = base + CLINT_IPI_OFF (offset 0). This layout definition is correct and consistent with the platform's device tree. Any fix to the MSIP write pattern (e.g., byte-store or read-modify-write) would operate on the same base address — no layout change needed. The init code itself does not perform MSIP writes.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:91-93  (defines IRQ_S_SOFT, IRQ_M_SOFT, IE_SIE)
      Defines IRQ_S_SOFT=1, IRQ_M_SOFT=3 (correct per privileged spec), and IE_SIE = (_AC(0x1, UL) << RV_IRQ_SOFT) at line 543 which is a single-bit mask for sip.SSIP/mip.MSIP. These CSR-side definitions are architecturally correct. The spec concern is about the memory-mapped MSIP register format, not the CSR interrupt pending bits. The IE_SIE mask correctly isolates the single defined interrupt bit in the sip/mip CSR.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/clint.h:1-27  (CLINT layout declaration)
      Header explicitly documents (lines 17-22) that the CLINT memory layout is implementation-defined beyond the ISA — confirming that non-ISA assumptions about MSIP register format are platform-specific. This directly supports the PR's premise that upper bits of MSIP are not architecturally specified and must not be unconditionally zeroed. The header is a correct architectural note, not a bug.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi-ipi.c:39-86  sbi_ipi_init
      When SBI IPI is selected (riscv_sbi_for_rfence static key enabled), the kernel delegates all IPI delivery to firmware via sbi_send_ipi(). Firmware internally writes the MSIP register. The kernel has zero visibility into whether the firmware masks writes to bit 0 or unconditionally writes full 32-bit values. An in-kernel fix to CLINT/ACLINT MSIP write patterns would leave this SBI-mediated path unaddressed — any masking deficiency in firmware propagates the same vulnerability.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kernel/sbi.c:190-202  __sbi_send_ipi_v02
      Passes 1UL as hart_mask to SBI firmware via ecall. Per the SBI v0.2+ specification, hart_mask is a bitmask (not a literal MSIP register value), so the kernel is using the SBI interface correctly at the API level. However, if a firmware implementation internally translates the hart_mask directly into a literal writel(hart_mask, msip) without masking to bit 0, the unmasked value reaches the hardware MSIP register through the firmware path. The kernel cannot observe or prevent this.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/cacheflush.c:21-44  flush_icache_all
      Properly places RISCV_FENCE(w,o) before IPI dispatch to order prior data stores before the MSIP MMIO write, as required by the RISC-V spec for remote fence.i correctness. The comment at line 34 explicitly acknowledges IPIs are triggered by MMIO writes to CLINT or S-IMSIC. The MSIP→mip reflection delay concern is a property of the underlying IPI transport, not this consumer — flush_icache_all correctly uses the standard kernel IPI interfaces. Any fix belongs in the MSIP write primitives, not here.
  [OK] arch/riscv/mm/tlbflush.c:118-150  __flush_tlb_range
      When riscv_use_sbi_for_rfence() is false (CLINT/ACLINT SSWI path), uses on_each_cpu_mask() which sends IPI via the ipi-mux → MSIP write path. The TLB flush ordering (flush local → IPI → remote flush) relies on the IPI mechanism's delivery guarantees. This is a correct consumer of the IPI infrastructure — it does not directly touch MSIP. The ordering dependency on MSIP→mip reflection timing is a property of the transport layer, fixed by addressing the MSIP write pattern, not by changing this caller.

