PR #1541  overall: bug_confirmed
Spec: Undefined memory-mapped msip register format allowed unmasked writes to bits 31:1 with unpredictable implementation-specific behavior, and unspecified write-to-CSR reflection delay created IPI race windows.
  [BUG] lib/utils/ipi/aclint_mswi.c:28-46  mswi_ipi_send
      writel_relaxed(1, ...) emits a full 32-bit 'sw' store to the memory-mapped msip register, whose architecturally defined width is exactly 1 bit (bit 0). Bits 31:1 are reserved/WARL per the ACLINT spec. On compliant implementations these bits ignore writes, but on legacy/vendor CLINT implementations where bits 31:1 were historically architecturally undefined, driving them as 0 on the bus may trigger vendor-specific side effects (e.g. unexpected IPI routing, interrupt controller state corruption). The code should use a byte write (writeb_relaxed) or mask to bit 0 only. No bitmask constant is defined in the header to enforce this.
  [RISK] lib/utils/ipi/aclint_mswi.c:48-61  mswi_ipi_clear
      writel_relaxed(0, ...) issues a full 32-bit write to clear the msip register. Writing 0 is benign on standard ACLINT implementations (WARL bits ignore zero writes), but the full-word access assumes the register is exactly 32 bits with no side effects on bits 31:1. On vendor CLINT implementations where upper bits were historically undefined, a full-word zero write could interact with implementation-defined state in those bits. The code itself is safe on compliant hardware, but correctness depends on the unvalidated assumption that all CLINT/ACLINT implementations treat a full-word zero write identically to a single-bit clear of bit 0.
  [BUG] lib/utils/ipi/aclint_mswi.c:70-113  aclint_mswi_cold_init
      Registers the entire MSWI MMIO region as SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_M_READABLE | SBI_DOMAIN_MEMREGION_M_WRITABLE in the root domain. This grants S-mode software read access to bits 31:1 of the msip register, which are reserved/WARL. On implementations where reading those bits returns non-zero values (e.g. microarchitectural state, implementation-specific flags), this creates an information leak path. The read access also enables S-mode TOCTOU races against M-mode IPI clearing. The region should be registered without M_READABLE, or only with M_WRITABLE access since MSIP is a write-only trigger from the M-mode perspective.
  [RISK] include/sbi_utils/ipi/aclint_mswi.h:15-27  (header definition)
      Defines ACLINT_MSWI_ALIGN, ACLINT_MSWI_SIZE, ACLINT_MSWI_MAX_HARTS, CLINT_MSWI_OFFSET and the aclint_mswi_data struct, but defines no bitmask constant for the MSIP register width (e.g. ACLINT_MSWI_MSIP_MASK = 0x1). Every consumer that writes to the msip register does so with a full 32-bit word because no narrower mask constant exists to guide correct usage. The 4-byte-per-hart layout assumption (sizeof(u32)) embedded in all consumers encodes a 32-bit access width without distinguishing between the register's physical width (32-bit aligned) and its logical width (1 defined bit). This missing abstraction is the root cause of every unmasked write in the codebase.
  [OK] include/sbi/riscv_io.h:26-29  __raw_writel
      __raw_writel emits a full 32-bit 'sw' instruction via inline asm — this is the correct general-purpose mechanism for 32-bit MMIO stores. The file also provides __raw_writeb for byte-sized MMIO stores, which exists as the correct alternative for narrow registers. The vulnerability is not in this primitive but in the caller's choice to use write{l,_relaxed} for the msip register instead of write{b,_relaxed}. The I/O layer correctly provides both widths; the bug is at the call site where the wrong width is selected.
  [BUG] lib/utils/ipi/fdt_ipi_mswi.c:16-51  ipi_mswi_cold_init
      Bridges legacy CLINT devices (riscv,clint0, sifive,clint0, thead,c900-clint) into the same aclint_mswi_cold_init() driver path that performs full-word MSIP writes. On legacy CLINT implementations, bits 31:1 of the msip register were architecturally undefined — they predate the ACLINT spec's WARL definition. The adjustment at lines 37-41 merely shifts the MMIO base to the MSWI sub-region but does not change the access width or bit-level semantics. This applies the unmasked 32-bit write pattern to hardware where the ISA manual historically left bits 31:1 completely unspecified, broadening the vulnerability from spec-compliant ACLINT to all pre-ACLINT CLINT implementations.
  [RISK] lib/utils/ipi/fdt_ipi_mswi.c:53-63  (match table)
      DT match table maps 'riscv,clint0', 'sifive,clint0', and 'thead,c900-clint' compatibles to CLINT_MSWI_OFFSET via the clint_offset variable. This table is a static data structure — the vulnerability is in how ipi_mswi_cold_init consumes it (fanning legacy CLINT devices into the unmasked ACLINT MSWI write path). The table itself is configuration, but it silently broadens the attack surface: any DT with a legacy CLINT compatible gets full-word MSIP writes applied to hardware where bits 31:1 have no architecturally guaranteed behavior. There is no warning, quirk flag, or platform-specific override that would signal 'this CLINT may not support full-word writes safely.'
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_ipi.c:47-96  sbi_ipi_send
      The atomic fetch_or gate (line 89-91) correctly prevents redundant MMIO writes when ipi_type was already non-zero, which is good. However, after sbi_ipi_raw_send() issues the writel_relaxed(1) to the remote hart's msip register, there is no read-back confirmation that the write propagated to mip.MSIP on the remote hart. On implementations where the interrupt controller has posted-write buffers or where mip.MSIP is asserted asynchronously, the remote hart may not observe the interrupt promptly. The correctness of the entire IPI dispatch chain depends on the unvalidated assumption that a relaxed MMIO store to the msip register immediately (or within a bounded time) asserts mip.MSIP on the target hart.
  [RISK] lib/sbi/sbi_ipi.c:249-272  sbi_ipi_process
      Calls sbi_ipi_raw_clear(false) (which issues wmb() after the MMIO write to clear msip) then immediately performs atomic_raw_xchg_ulong to consume pending IPI events. There is no read-back of the MMIO msip register or of mip.MSIP between the clear and the atomic exchange. On implementations with posted MMIO writes, the clear may not have propagated through the interrupt controller by the time events are processed, meaning a new IPI arriving during this window could be missed (the clear hasn't taken effect yet, so MSIP is still asserted, but the software flow has already consumed the ipi_type bitmap). The wmb() ensures write ordering but not completion.
  [OK] lib/sbi/sbi_ipi.c:274-302  sbi_ipi_raw_send
